# Fighting for Attachment

This is a rough season for me, for maintaining a drive/motivation to be alive, to thrive.

I want to be clear here, that I am absolutely _not_ experiencing any sort of ideation or impulse for any sort of self-harm, nor am I feeling like I no longer wish to live.

That being said, I’m accustomed to having much more drive/motivation to _be_ alive, much more to fill me with a _zest_ for life, than I currently do. This is a season where all the things that have, in the past, filled me with something akin to a joie de vivre, or a sense of purpose or at least engagement, or an appreciation for my life, feels like they're each, individually, under a systematic attack.

### National identity

I’ve had a deep disillusionment with my nation (the US)’s government and policies, but it's not until last year that I’ve begun to genuinely loathe, both my country, and also the bulk of the people in it. As jaded as I already was, it has still been unspeakably heartbreaking to watch my nation go from merely naïve, brash, posturing, and delusional, to actually unambiguously and cartoonishly evil. The current administration is hellbent on mimicking the worst depravities of every “evil” dictatorship I grew up distrusting and reviling. We still, thankfully, have many vestiges of our democratic republic still intact—but they are rapidly shriveling, and already permanently maimed to a level from which I do not believe we can sufficiently recover. Certainly, our national reputation has sufferred damage that would take decades to repair if we did a 180 _today_, (and I’m not convinced we still retain the _tools_ to repair it, even given a united will to do so).

I live in a country I wholeheartedly despise. It helps that I live in the urban Pacific Northwest, where we have a history of resistance to such loathsome ideologies, and can easily find others who feel exactly as I do. But not enough.

I feel trapped. I have been unsuccessful in finding a plausible means for me to expatriate (no—emmigrate), but I am convinced that it is becoming increasingly vital to my basic emotional health to do so, and may need to consider more drastic/IMplausible scenarios for leaving.

### Software engineering climate

My career of Software Engineering is in crisis. I’ve been at the same company, doing essentially the same work, for over 16 years, where previously I was motivated to shift jobs every two at the most. My current employer pays me well above what I believe I could make anywhere else, and I’ve been working remote full-time, for 14 of those 16 years.

I’ve been steadily losing my motivation and productivity at my job. The zeal is gone, and COVID 19, the obliteration of the last tattered remnants of our nation’s moral fiber, and traumatic events in my relationship to my ex-wife and two of my kids (one in particular, with whom I cut contact after he publicly posted detailed fantasies of murdering me), have not made it any easier to maintain my productivity, though I’ve been recovering slowly this year.

Insult to injury, I no longer feel that software- or web-development “feels like home”, as saturated with LLMs and “vibe coding” as it's become. Admittedly, at the current moment in history it’s difficult to tell for sure how permanent it is, especially at the current extremes. It’s abundantly clear to me that the current economic climate surrounding AI is a bubble, likely a worse one than the “dot com” one of a couple decades past. A lot of the hype and insanity around LLMs will drop along with the financial implosion, just as it did around the mass of ill-advised web businesses at that time. But, just as how the web itself, and the need to put absolutely everything on the web, did not somehow “vanish” with the ridiculous dot-com bubble that arose then, I do not think the importance of LLMs and its utility, especially in relation to dev work, is going to diminish, though the way we use it will likely change in some ways.

A year ago, six months ago, I’d have said vibe coding was a lot of bull. Since then, credible evidence has been mounting that it has become significantly more practical and advantageous, and I’m watching it produce increasingly useful results for industry veterans whom I trust and respect... though there are still plenty of hilarious examples that indicate that even “experts” have not quite learned how to use it responsibly. I think it’s here to stay. I think it will prove to be a genuinely valuable asset to the industry, as a productivity tool, though perhaps we will learn to treat it with far more caution and insanity.

...And I absolutely hate it. As the sentiment goes, I did not train in software engineering for decades of my life, only to become a “condom for Claude”. I live to be a coder—to solve puzzles, and forge solutions. Not a project manager, not a professional code reviewer, and not a voice of reason to redirect and guide an insane but extroardinarily-gifted infant with toxic positivity syndrome. I want the front lines, not a diplomat’s desk job. If you take from me the actual coding—or worse, the problem-solving, you have stolen from me everything that has made me love this career.

My current job has lost a lot of its flavor; I’ve simply been doing it for too long. But I can’t leave it—it’s one of the few that remain where I’m not (yet) under acute demand that I use LLMs for all my work. And, as I said, it pays more than anywhere else would, and I have unfortunately allowed my level of debt to catch up to my level of income—I live paycheck to paycheck, just to make payments on all the debt. This keeps me very much dependent on a job that allows me to afford doing just that. (This is also one of the several impediments to my ability to leave this country for a new home.)

### Family

As mentioned above, and also discussed in another previous post, I cut contact with one of my kids, my oldest son, after he wrote about “reveling in my demise” as he watches the light leave my eyes, and fantasies/impulses to “open [my] chest with an axe”. He wrote these things in response to my stated choice to heavily distance myself from him a bit prior to that, due to a combination of toxic relationship patterns, and a complete and utter rewrite of his childhood, and past relationship with me, wherein a number of abusive words, acts, and patterns (on my part) were invented, and were being publicly described on social media. I love my son, and I still hope for his happiness, and, especially, peace. But I no longer have any inclination to attempt to be a part of healing his brokenness.

There is a Max-shaped hole in my heart that is never going to be completely filled. I will never accept him back into my life (it is not impossible, but it is so improbable that I consider it ludicrous to contemplate), and no one else will fill that spot. I have a similar person-shaped hole for my ex-wife, whom I eventually realized to be a deeply toxic, but not actually malicious person. Though I no longer love her, and certainly do not miss her, I still want her to live a happy life (away from me), and the love I once had for her, ran very deep indeed.

My relationship with my second child, my daughter, has always been somewhat strained. Partly because our personalities are just too alike, and partly because some of her core life philosophies run pretty contrary to mine. I’ve no interest in trying to impose mine upon her... but neither does it make me seek a deeper relationship with her.

As described in a recent post, I’ve recently come to understand that, much like her older brother, she harbors false narratives of abusive patterns—though in her case it’s somewhat-saner/milder patterns involving emotional/verbal abuse—still very damaging, but at least they don’t include the accusations of physical abuse (apart from spanking, an unfortunate cultural norm here in the US that took me a while to shed) and sexual inappropriateness, that her older brother has invented.

In her case, I don’t believe these narratives to have been invented wholesale after the fact, but to be distortions of perception, stemming from a time when she was confusing my responses to her behaviors for being responses to who she is at her core—who she is as a person. This seems to have driven a wedge between us, that was wholly invisible to me until only now. If I’d known then, I would have been better armed to make her understand how loved she is. I can’t fix what no one alerts me to, and she’s kept it close to her chest for too many years.

Every day, I woke up asking myself how I could more effectively convey my love to my kids. I wanted literally nothing more than for my kids to (a) grow up learning how not to be an asshole, and (b) to feel absolutely, utterly safe with me, willing to confide anything, without fear of repurcussions. It is indescribably painful to learn that 2 out of 3 of my kids see me as NOT having been an emotional safe space, when all of my energy went to trying to be that for them.

My oldest, he did see me as safe, and his best friend, and did indeed confide absolutely _everything_ to me. His current views are very much an after-the-fact fabrication, a series of pathological constructions. These facts are, however, a hollow comfort, and are no consolation in the face of knowing that 2 of my 3 adult kids view me as a source of their trauma.

Being a great dad, raising kids that felt supported and emotionally secure, has meant a great deal to me. In the wake of those recent conversations with my daughter, it’s hard not to feel like a failure on that front, even if I know at the core of my heart, that there is a level of distortion to some of the memories she’s cited. It hurts worse, knowing there’s enough of at least a kernel of truth to most of her accusations, and knowing I genuinely did hurt her in some ways through ignorance. It’s hard not to feel like 27 years of fatherhood have been wasted time and energy, better undertaken by someone else. At some level I know it’s untrue, and that in many ways I did an amazingly awesome job. But being the only one to know it, aside from friends and family who, inevitably, were never as close to it as my kids and my own self, o
